N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition; this is the compositional audit. Every bar totals four eighths (2/4). Bar 1 D4 E4 quarters; bar 2 G4 E4 quarters; bar 3 A4 half note; bar 4 a two-beat rest; bar 5 A4 G4; bar 6 E4 D4; bar 7 E4 G4 (quarters); bar 8 G4 half note. Pitch set D-E-G-A, range D4-A4, largest interval a perfect fourth (E4-A4, bars 2-3); otherwise steps and minor thirds. The earlier draft shared its E4-G4 two-note shape, six sung notes and one unsung note with "Silver Drop"; this version is a continuous held-tone song with a four-bar answering phrase (the SK "longer phrase"), while "Silver Drop" is now short eighth-note drops and silent gaps. The analyzer reads level 2 (pentatonic, range within a sixth, a rest); its Ontario line names half notes (Grade 2), low so and la (Grade 3) and a one-sharp key signature (Grade 4) as notation concepts, but SK children learn by rote and only speed, direction, breath and silence are claimed. Originality: the so,-la,-do opening is common pentatonic property; checked against "Bounce High, Bounce Low", "Rain, Rain", "Lucy Locket", "Hot Cross Buns" and "Twinkle, Twinkle", with no shared opening. "Tōri" is unmetred solo shakuhachi music; nothing of it is used.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether SK children can sing the four-bar answer in one breath at quarter = 72, whether the drone helps or covers the breath, whether "Tōri" is the most suitable Riley Lee recording for a five-year-old's listening, and how a honkyoku player would want the tradition described. A classroom trial, a shakuhachi-informed educator and a second score reader must settle these.
